Here is an example of some code which shows how to do row selection in a grid view.  The technique being proposed is to override the DoMouseCommand function in your subclass of the TGridView.  In our example we implement a derivation of the TTextGridView but you could use the same technique for any descendant of TGridView.Our override of DoMouseCommand simply uses a TRowSelectCommand rather than the normal TCellSelectCommand.  But there is one catch: your grid view in the resource file must also be marked as Multiple Selections rather than as Single Selections.  (If you forget to do this then you will find that clicking anywhere in the row will select the last cell in that row.)The side effect of having Multiple Selection is that multiple rows can be selected by using the shift or command keys.  If you don't want this behavior then you will need to create your own version of TRowSelectCommand with a custom implementation of ComputeNewSelection.  If you take a look at TRCSelectCommand (from which TRowSelectCommand descends) in the ComputeNewSelection function you will see some code which checks fSingleSelection.  You should replace the single line in there after the 'if' with one that sets 'r' to be the entire row.  I.e.:  r = CRect(1, clickedCell.v, clickedCell.h + 1, clickedCell.v + 1);An alternate technique is to create a behavior which has the DoMouseCommand code we have used in TRowSelectGridView.  This behavior then could be attached to any grid view.  The advantage of this approach is that no specialization of your grid view class is required.  However, due to the fact that you have to subclass most of the TGridView class anyway (in order to provide your own DrawCell or GetText functions) you can save the overhead of yet another class (the behavior class) by using the technique proposed here.